\section{Sharing}{

A collection is shared, like \code{\link[shiny:reactiveValues]{shiny::reactiveValues()}}: after \code{y <- x}, both
names refer to the same collection, and a change made through \verb{$<-},
\verb{[[<-}, \verb{[<-}, \verb{names<-} or \code{reorder()} is seen by everything holding it.
That is what lets one part of an app change a collection while another
reacts to it. Subsetting with \code{[} returns a new collection with its own set
of slots, holding the same reactives, and \code{x[]} copies the whole collection.
So \code{x <- x[order]} gives a reordered copy, which suffices when nothing else
holds \code{x}, while \code{reorder(x, order)} reorders the shared collection itself.
Renaming with \verb{names<-} keeps every slot at its position, as for a list.

A copy made with \code{[} is shallow: its slots hold the same reactives as the
original, so after \code{y <- x["a"]}, calling \code{y$a(10)} also sets the value of
\code{x$a()}. A deep copy, made with \code{copy(x, deep = TRUE)}, gives each
\code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} slot a new \code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} holding the slot's
current value, so writing through a slot of the copy leaves the original
unchanged. Any other slot, such as a \code{\link[shiny:reactive]{shiny::reactive()}}, stays shared,
since it can't be copied and can't be written through either. A computed
slot that reads slots of the original keeps reading the original, not the
copy. By default, \code{copy()} makes the same shallow copy as \code{x[]}.
}

\section{Lifetime}{

A collection belongs to the session or module it was created in and is
destroyed along with it, as a \code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} is. Reading or changing
it from another module doesn't tie it to that module, so destroying the
module leaves the collection intact. A reactive bound to a slot, however,
still belongs to the module it was created in. A copy belongs to the session
or module it is made in, and so does each new \code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} of a
deep copy.

To keep reading a collection after its \code{\link[shiny:testServer]{shiny::testServer()}} session has
closed, a test can take a snapshot with \code{snapshot_reactives()} while the
session is still open. A snapshot belongs to no session. For each
\code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} slot it holds a new \code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} with the
slot's current value, and for each other slot a \code{\link[shiny:reactive]{shiny::reactive()}}
returning its current value, so writing through a slot of the snapshot or
of the original leaves the other unchanged. A slot that fails when computed
fails the same way when its copy is called, rather than failing the
snapshot. Names, order and class carry over.
}

\section{Dependencies}{

Reading a slot by name makes the caller depend on that slot alone. The
caller re-runs when the slot is bound, replaced or removed, but not when
other slots change. This also holds for a slot that does not exist yet, so a
reader re-runs once the slot is added. Subsetting with \code{[} by name depends
on the named slots in the same way.

The \code{length()} method depends on the number of slots alone, and \code{names()}
on which slots exist, their names and their order. Reading a slot by
position, as in \code{x[[1]]}, depends on what \code{names()} depends on and on the
slot it finds, so the caller re-runs whenever a slot is added, removed,
renamed or moved, even if the slot at its position stays the same. Reading
past the last slot fails, and the caller re-runs on the same changes.
Subsetting by position depends on the selected slots and on what \code{names()}
depends on. The \code{as.list()} method depends on what \code{names()} depends on and
on every slot, and \code{slot_values()} also on every slot's value. Copying, with
\code{x[]} or \code{copy()}, has the same dependencies as \code{as.list()}. A deep copy
reads the values it copies without depending on them, and it runs no
computed slot. Reordering re-runs readers of \code{names()}, \code{as.list()},
\code{slot_values()} and of slots and subsets by position, but not readers of
\code{length()} or of slots by name.

To depend on the slot at a position alone, read it through a
\code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}}: after \code{first <- reactiveVal()} and
\code{observe(first(x[[1]]))}, a reader of \code{first()} re-runs only when position 1
holds a different reactive, since writing a \code{\link[shiny:reactiveVal]{shiny::reactiveVal()}} the value
it already holds invalidates nothing.

Printing, \code{format()} and \code{str()} make the caller depend on nothing, and they
never call a slot, so they run no computed slot. Taking a snapshot also
makes the caller depend on nothing, though it runs every computed slot.
Outside a reactive consumer, as at the console, \code{names()} and \code{length()}
work as they would inside \code{\link[shiny:isolate]{shiny::isolate()}} rather than fail.
}

\section{Reactlog}{

In \href{https://rstudio.github.io/reactlog/}{reactlog}, the dependency on slot
\code{a} shows as \code{reactives$a}, or as \code{reactive_vals$a} in a \code{reactive_vals}
collection, the one on which slots exist and in what order as
\code{names(reactives)}, and the one on their number as \code{length(reactives)}.
Unnamed slots show as \code{reactives$...1}, \code{reactives$...2} and so on, numbered
in the order they were added rather than by position, so a label survives
reordering. A read of every slot at once, such as \code{as.list()}, shows a
single dependency on \code{reactives[]} instead of one per slot. The entry for a
slot only appears once it has been read, and the one for the number of
slots once \code{length()} has been called.
}

\examples{
x <- reactives(a = shiny::reactiveVal(1), b = shiny::reactive(2 * 21))
shiny::isolate(x$a())
shiny::isolate(x$b())

